@ id: rs02
@ order: 31
@ title: Why local CV misleads — the sparse-label trap
@ subtitle: a detector can look better locally than it really is
@ source: research/official_repo/src/tracking_cellmot/metrics.py

--- note
## The trap that wastes weeks
Our golden-12 is leak-free (rs01) — but it can still **rank two detectors wrong**. The reason is
the sparse-label adjustment in the metric (me01): FP edges among **unlabelled** cells are ignored.
So a detector that over-predicts into unlabelled space pays **no local penalty** — but it *does*
pay on the real (differently-labelled) test.

--- note
### The proof — canqiang inverts
**[Data — measured, not guessed]** We ran a second public detector, `canqiang`. It is **denser**
than pilkwang. On our local metric it scores **higher**; on the real LB it scores **lower**. The
ranking is **inverted**:
--- output
| detector | golden-12 (local) | public LB | agree? |
|---|---|---|---|
| pilkwang | 0.870 | **0.885** | — |
| canqiang | **0.903** | 0.866 | ❌ inverted |

--- note
### Why — density is free locally
**[Domain]** canqiang predicts many cells; the extra ones land in unlabelled regions → the metric
**ignores** them locally → its score inflates. On the real test (a different, unseen embryo) those
extra predictions become real errors. **A denser detector always looks better locally than it is.**
--- code
# the metric only judges edges touching a GT-tracked node (me01):
n_valid_pred_edges = int(edge_attrs["pred_valid"].sum())   # ignores FP in unlabelled space
# => predicting MORE cells cannot hurt the local score, but can hurt the LB
--- shape
dense → inflated local<br>→ local CV can invert<br>→ **Kaggle LB is truth**

--- note
### The rule we adopted
**[Craft]** Trust local CV for **relative** tweaks to the *same* precise detector (it's honest
there). Do **not** trust it to rank a *denser* detector higher — validate density changes on the
**Kaggle LB**. This single lesson saved us from chasing a mirage.
--- shape
same detector → trust local<br>denser detector → trust LB

